From 45d5a83b02e96b6b4791aa77c626cd52ca8daedb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tim Besard <tim.besard@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 13:57:28 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Fully unroll short loops with barriers by default

LLVM's unroller fully unrolls a short loop with a constant trip count on
its own, but not when the loop contains a barrier: pocl.barrier is
noduplicate. Such a loop becomes a b-loop instead, with context arrays for
the values live across the barriers and extra barriers around the loop
control, which costs far more than the copies of a short body.

Have UnrollBarrierLoops also fully unroll loops without an unroll request
when LLVM's static full unroll check accepts them: the unrolled size
estimate, with the unrolling preferences of the O3 pipeline the pass runs
in, has to stay below the full unroll threshold. The simplification bonus
of LLVM's dynamic cost analysis is not applied. '#pragma nounroll' and
partial unroll requests still keep the loop; requested loops keep the
larger pragma threshold.

The compiler unit test for a plain sub-group butterfly now checks that it
is unrolled, and new ones check that '#pragma nounroll' and a loop over the
size threshold stay b-loops. cond_barriers_in_for, test_issue_231 and
test_issue_553 cover b-loop handling with loops of 2 and 10 iterations
that would now be unrolled, so they get '#pragma nounroll'; so does
for_bug, which only stays over the threshold because of its printf.
